---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CsvToJson from '@/components/tools/data/CsvToJson.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="csv-to-json">
  <CsvToJson client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to convert a CSV file to JSON</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Load your data. Paste the CSV into the left pane, or drag a <code>.csv</code>, <code>.tsv</code> or <code>.txt</code> file onto it. The tool parses it in this browser tab.</li>
      <li>Check the delimiter. Auto-detect reads the first rows and picks the separator that gives a consistent column count. Change it if your file uses an unusual one.</li>
      <li>Decide how the keys are built. Keep “First row is a header” on to use row 1 as the JSON keys, or turn it off to get generated <code>column_1</code>, <code>column_2</code> names.</li>
      <li>Choose the output shape. An array of objects suits most APIs; column arrays suit plotting libraries; arrays of arrays keep the raw grid.</li>
      <li>Copy or download. The JSON updates as you type. Use Copy to paste it, or Download for a <code>.json</code> file.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Type inference: what changes and what does not</h2>
    <p>
      CSV has no types: each cell is text. With <strong>Infer value types</strong> on, the tool converts
      cells whose type is clear and keeps the others as text. Converters that do not make this
      distinction often corrupt data.
    </p>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-max border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th class="border-b px-3 py-2 text-left font-semibold">CSV cell</th>
            <th class="border-b px-3 py-2 text-left font-semibold">JSON value</th>
            <th class="border-b px-3 py-2 text-left font-semibold">Why</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>42</code></td>
            <td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>42</code></td>
            <td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Plain integer, safely representable</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>3.14</code></td>
            <td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>3.14</code></td>
            <td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Decimal number</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>007</code></td>
            <td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>"007"</code></td>
            <td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Leading zeros have meaning: usually a code, not a number</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>4916338506082832</code></td>
            <td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>"4916338506082832"</code></td>
            <td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Over 15 digits: JavaScript numbers would lose the last digits</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>true</code></td>
            <td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>true</code></td>
            <td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Boolean literal (also <code>TRUE</code> and <code>True</code>)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="border-b px-3 py-2">(empty)</td>
            <td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>null</code></td>
            <td class="border-b px-3 py-2">A missing value, not an empty string</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>2026-08-16</code></td>
            <td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>"2026-08-16"</code></td>
            <td class="border-b px-3 py-2">JSON has no date type; ISO text round-trips everywhere</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Building nested JSON from flat columns</h2>
    <p>
      Spreadsheets are flat; APIs are not. With <strong>Expand dotted keys</strong> on, a header path
      becomes structure: a column called <code>profile.city</code> becomes a nested object, and a numeric
      segment becomes an array index.
    </p>
    <pre><code>id,name,profile.city,tags.0,tags.1
1,Ada,London,analysis,mathematics</code></pre>
    <p>becomes</p>
    <pre><code>[
  &#123;
    "id": 1,
    "name": "Ada",
    "profile": &#123; "city": "London" &#125;,
    "tags": ["analysis", "mathematics"]
  &#125;
]</code></pre>

    <h2>Quoting, embedded commas and line breaks</h2>
    <p>
      The parser follows RFC 4180. A field in double quotes can contain the delimiter, newlines and
      escaped quotes written as <code>""</code>. So <code>1,"Doe, Jane","She said ""hi"""</code> parses
      to three fields, and the last one contains <code>She said "hi"</code>. Rows with a different
      number of fields than the header are still converted, and each one is listed as a warning, so you
      can find a malformed export before you import it.
    </p>

    <h2>Why do this in the browser?</h2>
    <p>
      CSV exports are often very sensitive files: payroll runs, customer lists, transaction logs. Here the
      file is read with the FileReader API, parsed by Papa Parse and rendered in the tab, so it is not
      uploaded. The only file size limit is the memory of your device. When the page has loaded, it works
      with the network turned off.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
